Differential Equations — MIT 18.03SC (@th-ode: 0, is #reference, #part-of @th-sources) { r_1VJHPM3DQ0PWYNFYKD11EKGCZE
Arthur Mattuck, Haynes Miller, Jeremy Orloff and John Lewis. MIT 18.03SC Differential Equations, Fall 2011. [Complete public course](https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-03sc-differential-equations-fall-2011/).

Use the complete first-order, second-order, Fourier/Laplace and systems progression, including problem-solving videos, assignments and independent examination attempts. Connect it to actual cooling, thermal-network, periodic-heating and stability models. Read the full needed calculus development through [Calculus and differential-equation preparation — Strang and Herman, OpenStax](../../../chemistry/industrial-chemistry/sources/Calculus-and-Differential-Equations-OpenStax.lingua#r_027WKVM5NG5X84M86TSVRA77J3) before skipping unexplained algebra or derivatives.

The course supplies mathematical understanding, while thermal conservation, constitutive relations and experimental validation establish the application. OCW's CC BY-NC-SA terms retain component exceptions. No course, examination solution, external software or lecture media is bundled.

**Return:** [Thermodynamics and heat transfer sources](../Sources.lingua#r_4N96T70VYW3C3M91M2SXF5KEQF).
